      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, authorial reputation significantly influences perceived intellectual authority, particularly in fast-moving academic fields. An author known for groundbreaking work carries a built-in credibility that can bias the reader toward viewing their content as more foundational. However, the *content* must ultimately support the authority; if the methodology is weak, even a famous name cannot salvage the argument. In a blind review, the rigor of the internal evidence would speak for itself.",
      "source": "target",
