      "content": "fair enough. but u mentioned blind reviews. honestly though, in the real world, reviewers often have subconscious biases about which institutions or even which types of scholars are \"leading\" the discourse. if u had to be totally honest and look at how these specific abstracts compared specifically regarding the *perceived* weight of the authors' backgrounds—given that Sterling sounds like a legacy academic and Adeyemi's profile might suggest a different, perhaps more localized or emerging research context—does that shift how a peer reviewer might subconsciously weigh their \"authority\"?",
